// Gama-G core: a declared recovery policy, executed rather than described.
//
//   ggc run examples/core/recover.gg --audit /tmp/recover.jsonl
//
// Spec section 10 makes recovery a language capability with six levels and asks
// two things that are easy to write down: that it never silently invents state,
// and that every action is observable and auditable. Section 18 asks that a
// failure before commit leave a recovery state rather than a half-applied change.
//
// The compiler hands this policy to the runtime *as a policy* -- a GIR protected
// region carrying these very steps -- and captures the `admission` checkpoint
// before any work runs, so `restore checkpoint admission` returns to admission
// rather than to whatever was recorded most recently. The transition at the end
// is emitted as a transaction, so a constraint failing there is recorded as an
// abort. Change `reading` to 500 and watch the whole policy run in the audit log.

gama core 0.2

intent Vitals
    purpose    record one observation, and recover if the reading is implausible
    authority  PatientWrite, AuditWrite
    checkpoint admission
    recover
        retry within 2 rounds
        restore checkpoint admission
        escalate operator
    trail      vitals recording

source reading : I64 from 72

// The state at its initial value is what `admission` records, so a restore puts
// the program back here rather than somewhere the policy never reasoned about.
state recorded : I64
    starts 0

operation Plausible
    uses     reading
    yields   checked : I64
    effect   pure
    holds    checked > 0
    computes reading

// A transition is the only way a state changes, it runs after every derivation,
// and it is the only thing inside the transaction.
transition Record
    alters   recorded
    uses     checked
    effect   audit
    needs    AuditWrite
    holds    checked <= 200
    computes recorded + checked
    trail    observation recorded

outcome recorded
